% A window device is driven, and a page device replaced under it.
%
% The window devices are the one part of the device fleet the suite could
% not run: they need a display, and a test that opened a window would put
% it on the display of whoever ran the suite. This one goes through
% xvfb-run, which conjures a display of its own, so the fleet is reachable
% here at all -- which is what the deferred window-device work needs
% before any of it can be measured rather than reasoned about.
%
% What is driven is the sequence a window device is retired by: paint on
% one, replace the page device so a second is made and the first retired,
% and paint on the second. Nothing about the outcome is subtle; the point
% is that it runs, on a real window, with a real display behind it.
%
% What this does NOT check: which device the context names for events.
% That pair is the context's own and no operator reads it, so a window
% retiring and clearing the wrong device's handler would not fail here.
% It is checked by reading the source and by the ordering in
% data/init.ps; making it observable wants an accessor this does not add.
%
% Prints SUCCESS iff both pages painted and the replacement completed.

/failures 0 def
% bool (name) . -    the name is only wanted when the bool is false
/check {
    exch { pop }{ (FAIL: ) print == /failures failures 1 add def } ifelse
} bind def

{ 0 0 40 40 rectfill } stopped not (a window device takes a fill) check
{ << /PageSize [90 90] >> setpagedevice } stopped not
    (the page device is replaced under a window device) check
{ 0 0 30 30 rectfill } stopped not
    (the device that replaced it takes a fill) check
{ showpage } stopped not (the page is shown) check

failures 0 eq { (a window device was driven and replaced: SUCCESS) = }
             { failures =print ( failures\n) print } ifelse
flush
quit
